The ambient (libbitrt) collector DOES fire under `BIT_GC=stress` — this was
wrong before #4160. Measured over 8 runs: default-policy runs report
`collections=0` every time; `BIT_GC=stress` runs report ~15,840-15,865, never
zero. The STEPS=600 loop takes a real safepoint on every back-edge, and once
`BIT_GC=stress` is set each one is a real ambient collection — see the header
comment's #4097 note above, which is the mechanism.

This program is still exempt from the harness's stress/default collection-
count proof, but not because the ambient collector never runs. It is exempt
because every quantity this program asserts on — numObjects/liveBytes/
collections/totalSwept/survivors/indexEntries — is read out of the PRIVATE
`gcBindMemory`-bound collector state in `g`, never out of anything the
ambient collector scans or sweeps. Whether the ambient collector ran 0 times
or 15,865 times cannot move a single recorded value.

The one thing an ambient collection COULD break — `arena`/`indexArena`/
`heapBlock`/`state`/`roots`/`meta` being swept once `gcBindMemory` has taken
their raw addresses and nothing in `main` reads them by name again — is
checked directly, not by the generic collection-count proof: `alive`, on the
final printed line, reads every one of their lengths, and the header's #4097
note records that each of the two `sizeRoundTrip`/`refArrayTrace` arms
segfaults under `BIT_GC=stress` without that read and passes with it. That is
the actual rooting property under test here, and it already has its own
oracle.
